 "use strict";
 const library=window.JASPER_FANFIC_DATA;
 if(!library||!Array.isArray(library.series))return;
 const revisions={"after-always":{"after-always-48":{"number":48,"title":"The Lantern Vigil","content":"Severus stood beneath the far arch with his coat buttoned to the throat and his hands in his pockets.\n\nI spotted him because everyone else was moving.\n\nThe Frostturn Lantern Walk had drawn people from every district, though not everyone had come for the same reason. Some carried lights painted with names. Some wore their best coats. Others had arrived straight from work, their boots still muddy from the ridge path. A child had brought a lantern shaped like an extremely unconvincing duck.\n\nGreg was playing a slow instrumental piece near the fountain.\n\nFor once, he hadn't been asked to fill every silence. The notes sat lightly in the air, leaving spaces for the sound of water beneath the old stones. People could listen, talk quietly, or ignore the music altogether.\n\nSeverus was doing none of those things. He was watching the lanterns with an expression I couldn't read.\n\nI started toward him.\n\nThen I stopped.\n\nWe had spent months learning that affection did not make another person's solitude a problem for me to solve. It would be easy to walk over and decide his silence was an invitation simply because I wanted to be beside him.\n\nI approached slowly enough that he could see me.\n\n\"Would you like company?\" I asked.\n\nHe glanced at me.\n\n\"That depends.\"\n\n\"On?\"\n\n\"Whether you intend to describe the emotional significance of every lantern.\"\n\n\"I was going to start with the duck.\"\n\nHe looked across the square.\n\n\"That creature is anatomically offensive.\"\n\nI smiled.\n\n\"Can I stand here?\"\n\nHe moved slightly to make room.\n\nIt was a small answer. It was enough.\n\nWe watched the procession begin. Neri checked the safe route, Edda directed people away from the steps still closed after the storm, and Pip made sure the temporary lantern stands did not narrow the walkway. The lower passage toward Tideglass remained under inspection. Nobody treated the festival as permission to ignore a closure.\n\nGreg's song changed key.\n\nSeverus's fingers twitched inside his pocket.\n\n\"Do you know the piece?\" I asked.\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Do you like it?\"\n\nHe considered.\n\n\"It doesn't insist on being admired.\"\n\nI laughed softly.\n\n\"Greg would probably consider that high praise.\"\n\n\"Then please don't tell him.\"\n\nThe air smelled of cold stone, candle smoke, and the citrus tea Iroh was serving from a stand near the bakery. A lantern moved past us carrying a painted blue wave. Another held no mark at all.\n\nI thought of the third wartime letter Severus had chosen not to open.\n\n\"Are you remembering someone?\" I asked, then immediately wished I'd phrased it differently.\n\nHe looked at me.\n\n\"Several people.\"\n\n\"You don't have to tell me who.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\nWe stood quietly.\n\nAfter a while he said, \"For much of my life, remembrance was a public examination. People expected grief to resemble guilt, or loyalty, or confession. They wanted a correct answer.\"\n\n\"And here?\"\n\n\"Here there is a duck.\"\n\nI snorted.\n\nHe glanced at me.\n\n\"An important improvement.\"\n\nI covered my mouth.\n\nHe finally smiled.\n\nA small disagreement interrupted the line before it reached the corner.\n\nTwo volunteers had placed a lantern stand across the paved turning space outside the tea stall. Nobody had done it carelessly; the wind was stronger there, and the stand looked secure against the wall. But Edda stopped when she reached it.\n\n\"I can't turn around that,\" she said.\n\nOne of the volunteers looked toward the steps.\n\n\"We can carry it past you.\"\n\n\"I don't need to be carried past it,\" Edda answered. \"The stand needs to move.\"\n\nSeverus watched the conversation with his hands still in his pockets.\n\nThe volunteer flushed. \"We thought the space was wide enough.\"\n\n\"Then measure it,\" Edda said.\n\nPip fetched the chalk and a ruler, and the group moved the stand to a spot where it could be safely tethered without narrowing the route. It took less than ten minutes. The rest of the procession simply waited, talking quietly while Greg played the last notes of his piece.\n\nSeverus tilted his head.\n\n\"They corrected the design without declaring anyone incompetent.\"\n\n\"You're sounding impressed.\"\n\n\"I am assessing an interesting deviation from school staff meetings.\"\n\nI laughed.\n\nHe looked at Edda, who had resumed her place in the line on her own terms.\n\n\"When I taught,\" he said after a moment, \"I sometimes mistook embarrassment for a useful lesson.\"\n\nI let the admission stand. I could have asked for a list of his regrets. Instead I asked whether he wanted another cup of tea.\n\n\"Later,\" he said. \"I should like to see whether this ridiculous bird-shaped lantern survives the wind.\"\n\nIt did. A child held it with both hands and grinned as though the survival was entirely their own achievement.\n\nSeverus allowed himself a small smile.\n\nI loved that he had seen the practical work and what it meant, without needing the entire evening to become a public confession.\n\nThe procession moved toward Jasmine Steps. Greg stopped playing and helped carry a lantern stand to the next corner. He had volunteered for one shift, not an entire night of unpaid music. I watched him exchange a few words with Iroh, then return to the square without a guitar in his hands.\n\nSeverus followed my gaze.\n\n\"Your other companions appear to be managing.\"\n\n\"They are.\"\n\nHe nodded.\n\nI waited for the old sharp remark. It didn't come.\n\nWe had spoken about my ethical non-monogamy and preference for polyamory often enough that he no longer treated every shared public event as a test of whether I would choose him above someone else. But understanding didn't erase the need for small negotiations.\n\n\"Do you want to walk with me?\" I asked. \"Or would you rather stay here?\"\n\nHe looked down the lit path.\n\n\"Walk. Slowly.\"\n\nWe joined the back of the procession. Nobody asked him to carry a lantern. Nobody tried to make him speak about the war. We passed the workshop lights at Brasswatch, then turned toward the safe ridge above Tideglass.\n\nMaui was helping a family settle blankets on a bench. He raised a hand in greeting but didn't call us over.\n\nSeverus noticed.\n\n\"He's learning restraint.\"\n\n\"Painfully.\"\n\n\"A remarkable achievement.\"\n\nI smiled.\n\nAt the cove, I hung a small lantern with an open amber circle beside the others. It wasn't mine to explain. Severus watched me place it on the hook and step back.\n\n\"You left it blank,\" he said.\n\n\"Not blank. Unnamed.\"\n\nHe considered that distinction.\n\n\"Very well.\"\n\nHe took a folded scrap of paper from his pocket. I pretended not to notice until he held it out.\n\nIt was a single line, written in his precise handwriting.\n\nFOR THOSE I FAILED TO ASK.\n\nI looked at him.\n\n\"You want this on a lantern?\"\n\n\"No.\" His voice was quiet. \"I wanted you to know I had something to say. I don't want it displayed.\"\n\nI folded the paper carefully and gave it back.\n\n\"Then it's yours.\"\n\nHe closed his fingers around it.\n\nThe wind off the sea was colder than it had been in the square. I shivered. Without comment, Severus opened the side of his cloak and drew me close enough to share its shelter.\n\n\"Is this acceptable?\" he asked.\n\n\"Very.\"\n\nHe looked down at me.\n\n\"You appear to be smiling.\"\n\n\"I am.\"\n\n\"An unwise reaction to such an undignified arrangement.\"\n\n\"Extremely.\"\n\nHis hand rested against my back. He leaned close, paused, and let me close the last distance myself. The kiss was quiet enough to belong to us, not the crowd.\n\nWhen we turned toward the path, Greg began another instrumental somewhere beyond the ridge.\n\nSeverus listened for a moment.\n\n\"Tell him,\" he said reluctantly, \"that the second piece was better.\"\n\n\"Would you like to tell him yourself?\"\n\nHe looked offended.\n\n\"Don't become ambitious.\"\n\nI laughed.\n\nThe night ended without a speech from Severus, a public confession, or a declaration that he'd been transformed by festival lights.\n\nHe walked home beside me, our hands brushing inside the shelter of his cloak.\n\nI didn't need the silence to mean anything more than he had chosen to share it.","word_count":1344}},"love-and-duty":{"love-and-duty-47":{"number":47,"title":"The Longlight Kites","content":"The festival committee wanted Aang to teach everybody an Air Nomad sacred ceremony.\n\nThat was how the request reached us.\n\nPip had written a polite letter asking whether the Avatar might \"share the beautiful flying traditions of his people\" at the Longlight Kite Festival. They hadn't intended harm. The committee liked bright fabrics, impressive demonstrations, and the idea that one famous person could make a local celebration feel important.\n\nAang read the letter twice.\n\nThen he set it down.\n\n\"That's not what kites are for,\" he said.\n\nToph leaned across the table.\n\n\"Usually they're for flying.\"\n\nKatara hid a smile.\n\nAang looked at her.\n\n\"Don't encourage her.\"\n\n\"I'm not.\"\n\n\"You are.\"\n\nI took the letter.\n\n\"What's wrong with the request?\"\n\nHe rubbed the back of his neck.\n\n\"Some Air Nomad practices are meant to be taught in specific ways, by people who understand their meaning. I can show children how to make a kite. I can't turn every tradition I remember into entertainment for a festival.\"\n\nThe words were quiet, but there was grief beneath them.\n\nThe Longlight Festival belonged to Lanternweald, not to the Air Nomads. It had begun when the valley's stewards needed visible wind markers along the ridges before the winter storms. Families made bright cloth shapes and attached them to safe poles so travelers could see where the gusts were strongest. Over the years the wind markers became kites. Some people decorated them with symbols from home, but nobody had a claim on anyone else's sacred practices.\n\nThe committee had forgotten the difference between sharing a skill and borrowing a culture.\n\n\"Then we tell them,\" I said.\n\nAang nodded.\n\n\"I'll teach kite-making. But not a ceremony that isn't mine to give away.\"\n\nWe went to the meeting together.\n\nPip looked mortified when Aang explained.\n\n\"I didn't mean to make a spectacle of your people,\" they said.\n\n\"I know,\" Aang answered. \"That's why I'm telling you.\"\n\nThe committee adjusted the program. The opening would explain the valley's own history of wind markers and safe travel routes. People could bring designs meaningful to them, but no one would be asked to display sacred symbols or explain private beliefs. Aang would teach balance, frame strength, and how to test a kite without endangering bystanders.\n\nTamsin approved the load limits. Fen marked the ridge where the wind was steady enough. Edda inspected the path to the viewing platform, and Danse offered to help secure the temporary railings.\n\nToph was suspicious.\n\n\"Are we doing another festival where the whole town thinks wood is indestructible?\"\n\n\"Not this time,\" I said.\n\n\"Good. My patience has limits.\"\n\nBy the next morning the square was full of frames and colored cloth.\n\nAang sat on the floor with six children and two adults, teaching them how to tie a bridle knot. He was patient when the first kite twisted sideways. He didn't take it away and fix it. He asked which part felt uneven and let the child examine the string.\n\nI thought of Lio's apprenticeship at Tideglass.\n\nThe lesson was spreading.\n\nKatara helped a family choose lighter fabric because their child couldn't hold a heavy frame for long. Toph tested the stones beneath the launching area and declared one section too loose for running. We marked it off.\n\nI made a kite with a pattern of small amber circles.\n\n\"Very original,\" Toph said.\n\n\"Thank you.\"\n\n\"It looks like a potato.\"\n\n\"That's cultural criticism, and I reject it.\"\n\nShe laughed.\n\nAang looked over.\n\n\"I like it.\"\n\n\"You would,\" Toph said. \"You like anything that flies.\"\n\n\"That's not true.\"\n\nShe raised an eyebrow.\n\nHe considered.\n\n\"All right. Mostly true.\"\n\nThe first launch went badly.\n\nMy kite rose, tilted left, and fell into a patch of soft grass. The string tangled around my wrist.\n\nAang came toward me.\n\n\"Need help?\"\n\n\"Yes. I need you to stop looking so pleased with yourself.\"\n\nHe grinned.\n\n\"That's not a repair.\"\n\n\"Can you help with the string?\"\n\nHe sat beside me and showed me how to adjust the balance point. He didn't make the knot for me. It took three tries.\n\nWhen the kite finally rose, the cloth caught the light. The amber circles shone against the sky.\n\nI felt absurdly happy.\n\nAang watched it.\n\n\"That's beautiful,\" he said.\n\n\"It's a potato.\"\n\n\"A very graceful potato.\"\n\nI laughed.\n\nAt the materials table, a boy named Lio arrived with a frame too heavy for the string he'd brought.\n\n\"I wanted it to be bigger than Maui's,\" he admitted.\n\nToph folded her arms.\n\n\"Then you wanted a kite or a monument?\"\n\nHe looked offended.\n\n\"Both.\"\n\nKatara knelt beside the frame and asked him to lift it as he would when the wind caught. He managed it for three seconds before his arms shook.\n\n\"Would you like to make a lighter version?\" she asked.\n\nLio looked toward Maui, who had been asked to hold a line at the far end of the field.\n\n\"Will he laugh?\"\n\n\"Absolutely,\" Toph said. \"Then we'll laugh at his when it falls.\"\n\nAang sat with us and showed Lio how to trim the crossbar without weakening the center joint. He didn't take over the design. Lio chose a smaller sail, kept the original painted symbol, and wrote down the dimensions in his notebook so he could teach the next person.\n\nWhen the kite finally lifted, Maui cheered so loudly that two others looked around.\n\nLio rolled his eyes. Then he grinned.\n\nI thought about the apprentices at Brasswatch and the first boats at Tideglass. Skill was traveling between the districts without requiring everybody to become the same sort of person.\n\nA volunteer came by to ask whether the event would run another hour. The sky was still bright, but Fen's wind board showed stronger gusts expected near dusk.\n\nAang checked the reading and shook his head.\n\n\"We close launches at the posted time. People can stay for food and music if they like.\"\n\nHe looked disappointed. He loved seeing the children fly, and I knew how much the afternoon meant to him.\n\n\"Are you okay?\" I asked.\n\n\"Yes,\" he said, then corrected himself. \"I'm sad to stop. That doesn't mean the rule is wrong.\"\n\nToph made a satisfied noise.\n\n\"Finally. An Avatar who understands weather isn't impressed by fame.\"\n\nHe stuck his tongue out at her.\n\nI laughed, then helped roll the unused string for the next festival.\n\nThe festival continued through the afternoon. Some kites never flew. Some tore in the gusts. One child painted a fierce red bird that spun in circles before landing directly on Maui's head.\n\nHe declared it an act of war.\n\nNobody believed him.\n\nNear sunset, Aang went to the edge of the ridge alone. I followed, leaving enough space for him to turn back if he wanted quiet.\n\n\"Mind company?\" I asked.\n\nHe shook his head.\n\nThe sky was pale gold. Kites moved above the valley like small living shapes, each with a different line and angle. The old Threadway arches glowed below us, not as trophies but as paths people could choose.\n\n\"I miss them,\" Aang said.\n\nI didn't need to ask who.\n\nHe looked at the kites.\n\n\"When people ask me to perform the traditions of the Air Nomads, part of me wants to say yes. I want them to know how beautiful my people were. I want the world to remember.\"\n\n\"And the other part?\"\n\n\"Doesn't want their memory reduced to a show.\"\n\nI took his hand.\n\n\"You get to decide what you share.\"\n\n\"Sometimes I feel selfish.\"\n\n\"Preserving meaning isn't selfish.\"\n\nHe looked at me.\n\n\"You sound like Katara.\"\n\n\"High compliment.\"\n\nHe smiled.\n\nWe stood together while the first evening lanterns came on.\n\n\"Can I ask you something?\" he said.\n\n\"Of course.\"\n\n\"Do you ever feel pulled between everyone you love?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"How do you know where you belong?\"\n\nI thought about the calendar at Riverstone, the quiet nights at Jasmine Steps, the workshop lights at Brasswatch, and the sea-glass orchard at Tideglass. I thought of Katara coming home, Toph choosing her own time, and Aang standing beside me with a history I could respect but never replace.\n\n\"I don't think belonging is a single place,\" I said. \"I think it's the choices we keep making honestly.\"\n\nHe leaned close.\n\n\"That's a good answer.\"\n\n\"Don't let Toph hear you.\"\n\n\"She'll say it's a terrible answer.\"\n\n\"Then she'll steal it.\"\n\nHe laughed.\n\nI kissed him under the first star, gentle and unhurried. The wind tugged at my hair and carried the sound of the festival up the ridge.\n\nLater we packed away the kites that could be reused and labeled the damaged ones for repair. Aang helped without being asked to perform anything sacred.\n\nAt home, Katara had made tea. Toph was complaining about the potato kite.\n\nI hung it by the window.\n\nIt looked ridiculous.\n\nIt was mine.","word_count":1469}},"what-the-armor-leaves-behind":{"what-the-armor-leaves-behind-47":{"number":47,"title":"A Practical Kind of Courage","content":"The trainee asked Danse why the workshop safety handbook had a section titled IDENTIFY UNTRUSTWORTHY PERSONNEL.\n\nThe question landed in the room like a dropped wrench.\n\nRenn was an adult apprentice from a border settlement who had come to Brasswatch for a short training placement. They were learning to maintain water pumps and repair simple metal fittings. They had proved patient with measurements, careful with tools, and completely uninterested in the idea that a former Paladin's reputation should end a conversation.\n\nDanse looked at the page.\n\n\"That section was copied from an older manual.\"\n\n\"Why is it still here?\" Renn asked.\n\nHe didn't answer immediately.\n\nI was at the next table checking the revised payment records. Ash was working in the adjoining room, where the private door now locked from the inside. They could hear the discussion, but nobody had asked them to participate.\n\nRenn tapped the page.\n\n\"It says unfamiliar people should be reported until their origin is confirmed. That sounds like a reason to watch everyone who comes through the Threadways.\"\n\nDanse rubbed a hand across his jaw.\n\n\"It was written for a military environment.\"\n\n\"Is the workshop a military environment?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Then why are we teaching it?\"\n\nI looked at him.\n\nHe met my eyes, then looked back at the trainee.\n\n\"You're right.\"\n\nRenn blinked.\n\n\"I am?\"\n\n\"Yes. That section doesn't belong in this handbook.\"\n\nIt could have ended there, with a simple correction and everyone returning to work.\n\nBut Danse was staring at the page with the expression of someone who had discovered an old voice speaking through his own handwriting.\n\n\"I used to believe those procedures were necessary,\" he said. \"I enforced them.\"\n\nRenn waited.\n\n\"That doesn't make them correct,\" Danse continued. \"And it doesn't make the people affected by them responsible for making me feel better about changing.\"\n\nI set down the ledger.\n\nIt wasn't a polished apology. It was better.\n\nRenn looked at him.\n\n\"Then can we write a new rule?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nThey pulled a blank sheet from the drawer.\n\nThe new section would concern actual safety behavior: handling hazardous materials, keeping exit routes clear, documenting threats without profiling visitors, asking consent before examining personal belongings, and protecting private information unless there was a specific, immediate danger.\n\nIt would not require anyone to disclose a homeworld, identity, or past allegiance merely to receive repair service.\n\nI helped check the wording.\n\n\"How do we make sure people actually follow it?\" Renn asked.\n\n\"Training,\" Danse said.\n\n\"And consequences,\" I added.\n\nHe nodded.\n\n\"Consequences.\"\n\nBy noon, we had revised three pages.\n\nThe work revealed other old assumptions. One instruction required the person in charge to approve every emergency exit. That made sense for a locked armory and no sense for a public workshop where people might need to leave during a fire.\n\nAnother said workers should ignore a task interruption until given permission to stop. That was how injuries happened.\n\nRenn crossed it out.\n\nDanse watched them.\n\n\"Are you angry?\" he asked.\n\n\"At the old handbook? Yes.\"\n\n\"At me?\"\n\nRenn thought about it.\n\n\"A little. But mostly I want to know whether this place will be different.\"\n\nDanse nodded.\n\n\"Then judge the changes we make.\"\n\nI could see how hard it was for him not to ask for forgiveness, not to explain his old reasoning until it sounded noble. He let the discomfort stay.\n\nBefore the test, Ash asked to read the revised handbook in the private workroom.\n\nRenn carried them a clean copy and waited outside while Ash looked through it. When they returned, two sections had been marked with dark pencil.\n\n\"You changed the question about where people come from,\" Ash said. \"But you still ask whether someone has a lawful reason to be here.\"\n\nDanse frowned.\n\n\"That line concerns workshop access.\"\n\n\"Then say workshop access. People can have an appointment or need an urgent repair without proving they belong in the valley.\"\n\nI watched Danse look at the sentence as though it had appeared on the page while he wasn't paying attention.\n\n\"You're right.\"\n\nHe crossed it out.\n\nRenn added a practical substitute: *Ask what service is needed and whether it can be provided safely. For hazardous equipment, explain specific training and access restrictions without demanding personal history.*\n\nAsh nodded.\n\n\"Better. And the complaint process?\"\n\nDanse showed them the line that directed complaints to the workshop supervisor.\n\n\"That's you,\" Ash said.\n\n\"It is.\"\n\n\"What happens if the complaint is about you?\"\n\nI saw the discomfort move across his face.\n\n\"Then it cannot stop with me.\"\n\nWe wrote a second path to the independent steward and included where to leave a sealed note. The procedure would be posted at the entrance, not hidden in the handbook after forty pages.\n\nDanse put the pencil down.\n\n\"I thought I was making the rules clearer.\"\n\n\"You are,\" Renn said. \"Because people can question them.\"\n\nAsh returned the copy without praising him.\n\nThey didn't owe him that.\n\nWhen the emergency exercise began, I noticed Danse left the marked copy on the front table where anyone could see the revisions. The old habit would have been to lock away a flawed procedure until it was perfect.\n\nToday the process itself was public, with a date beside the next review.\n\nIt was one more way this workshop differed from the place he'd once called home.\n\nThat afternoon we tested the new emergency procedure.\n\nPip played the role of someone arriving with a box of loose fittings and no appointment. Edda tested the public entrance. Renn simulated a small electrical fault using a disconnected training board. Ash chose to observe from the private workroom with the door closed.\n\nDanse did not order anyone to reveal where they came from.\n\nHe asked where help was needed.\n\nWhen Renn reported the simulated fault, the group used the accessible exit and left the tools behind. The test wasn't perfect. Someone had to move a cart out of the way. The alarm cord was too high for one visitor. We wrote both problems down.\n\n\"Again tomorrow,\" Danse said.\n\nRenn smiled.\n\n\"Good.\"\n\nAfter the shift, I found Danse alone in the storage shed looking at the old handbook. He had separated the discarded pages from those still useful. The paper smelled of oil and dust.\n\n\"Will you burn it?\" I asked.\n\nHe shook his head.\n\n\"I want to keep one copy as an example of what not to teach.\"\n\n\"With notes?\"\n\n\"With notes.\"\n\nI sat beside him.\n\n\"Does it feel strange?\"\n\n\"Very.\"\n\nHe looked down at the title.\n\n\"There was a time when I thought courage meant obeying the right order despite fear. Later I thought it meant refusing an order that was wrong. Today I had to admit that I was still teaching part of the wrong order without noticing.\"\n\n\"That's a different kind of courage.\"\n\n\"Not an exciting one.\"\n\n\"Most useful courage isn't.\"\n\nHe smiled at that.\n\nWe watched the evening settle over Brasswatch Yard. Workers were closing the safe storage shed, and the hand pump was being checked one last time before the night rota began.\n\n\"Do you think Renn will come back tomorrow?\" he asked.\n\n\"Probably. You paid them, listened, and let them correct you.\"\n\n\"That seems like a low standard.\"\n\n\"It's a beginning.\"\n\nHe took my hand.\n\n\"I want to build something people can trust.\"\n\n\"Then keep asking them what works.\"\n\nHe turned toward me.\n\n\"Do you trust me?\"\n\nThe question was quiet enough that I almost missed it.\n\n\"I trust you to tell me the truth about what you're trying to change,\" I said. \"And I trust myself to tell you when something still hurts. I don't need to pretend the past is harmless to love you now.\"\n\nHe looked at me for a long moment.\n\nThen he lifted my hand to his lips.\n\n\"Thank you.\"\n\n\"For what?\"\n\n\"For not calling it finished.\"\n\nI leaned close. He kissed me slowly, with none of the stiffness that had once made him hold every tender gesture like a military inspection. His hand rested gently against my cheek, and I smiled into the kiss.\n\n\"Come home,\" I whispered.\n\nHe nodded.\n\nWe left the old handbook on the table, marked for revision.\n\nThe following morning, Renn arrived with two new questions and a better drawing of the emergency exit.\n\nDanse greeted them at the door.\n\n\"Good morning,\" he said. \"What have I missed?\"\n\nRenn grinned.\n\n\"Plenty.\"\n\nHe held the door open.\n\n\"Then let's get to work.\"","word_count":1402}},"stars-between-the-strings":{"su-24":{"number":24,"title":"A Little More Honest","content":"Greg arrived for the clinic appointment carrying an umbrella with one bent spoke and the expression of a man about to audition for a comedy show.\n\nThe waiting room smelled of floor cleaner and damp coats. Someone had taped a hand-drawn sign to the drinking fountain asking people not to leave cups in the sink. Through the window I could see Beach City traffic moving slowly around a puddle.\n\n\"Great day to get rained on by a medical professional,\" Greg said.\n\n\"We're indoors.\"\n\n\"Details.\"\n\nHe had made the appointment three times and canceled twice. Today he had asked me to accompany him, which was different from asking me to manage it.\n\n\"Do you want me at the desk with you?\" I asked.\n\n\"No. I can do that.\"\n\nHe could. He gave the receptionist his information, checked the appointment time, and came back with a clipboard that looked strangely heavy in his hands.\n\n\"Everything okay?\" I asked.\n\n\"Yeah. Just a lot of little boxes.\"\n\nI glanced at the form.\n\n\"Would you like help reading any of it?\"\n\nHe shook his head, then nodded.\n\n\"Maybe the one about emergency contacts.\"\n\n\"That's yours to choose.\"\n\nHe looked at me.\n\n\"I know. I just keep thinking whoever I write down gets assigned a whole job without being asked.\"\n\n\"Then ask them before you decide.\"\n\nGreg sat beside me. He turned the form over and tried to call Steven, then stopped.\n\n\"He's got a lot going on.\"\n\n\"He can answer whether he wants to be listed. It's not the same thing as being on call every minute.\"\n\nHe made the call. Steven agreed, and Greg wrote his name with care. He asked whether I wanted to be an additional contact for visits when I was in Beach City. I told him yes, with the understanding that my travel through Lanternweald meant there would be times I wasn't reachable.\n\n\"Good,\" he said. \"Two people, two actual choices.\"\n\nThe receptionist called his name.\n\nAt the doorway, Greg turned back.\n\n\"You'll still be here?\"\n\n\"I'll be here when the appointment ends unless you tell me you'd rather go alone.\"\n\nHe smiled nervously and went inside.\n\nI did not follow. The waiting room held a rack of old magazines and a potted plant whose leaves looked as tired as I felt. I watched an older man return a library book to the woman beside him and apologize for bending the cover. She shrugged and said she was more interested in whether he'd enjoyed it.\n\nThat seemed sensible.\n\nWhen Greg came out, he was holding a card with a follow-up date.\n\n\"No apocalypse,\" he announced.\n\n\"That was never on the schedule.\"\n\n\"Then I'd like a refund for the suspense.\"\n\nThere was a shallow puddle across the direct path to the parking lot. Greg stepped toward it without looking, then caught himself when a woman using a walking stick asked the receptionist whether the side exit had a dry route.\n\nThe receptionist checked the weather notice.\n\n\"The front walk is safer today. The side path is being repaired.\"\n\nGreg moved back to make room rather than insisting he could help her across the water. I waited beside him. The woman thanked the receptionist and made her own way out.\n\nAfterward he looked at me.\n\n\"That was awkward.\"\n\n\"Why?\"\n\n\"I almost did the whole heroic thing, and she hadn't asked for a hero.\"\n\n\"You're allowed to learn.\"\n\n\"I thought I was good at helping.\"\n\n\"You are. Sometimes you're so busy being ready that you forget to ask what the actual help is.\"\n\nHe considered that as we walked to the van.\n\nI had wanted to fill every silence after the appointment with reassurance. Seeing him stop his own impulse reminded me to stop mine. We hadn't reached a verdict about his future, and a follow-up card didn't make me his guardian.\n\nIn the van he put the appointment papers into an envelope instead of leaving them on the dashboard where anyone passing could read them. He wrote the date on the outside.\n\n\"Look,\" he said. \"Organized.\"\n\n\"Don't let it go to your head.\"\n\n\"Too late.\"\n\nI laughed, and the tension finally eased.\n\nWe stepped under the awning. He looked relieved, but a little shaken.\n\n\"Do you want to tell me what they said?\" I asked.\n\n\"Some of it. Not the whole thing.\"\n\n\"Okay.\"\n\nHe told me about a routine maintenance check, a recommendation to keep the follow-up appointment, and a few things he'd need to pay attention to. He didn't give me every private detail. I didn't ask for them.\n\nThen he stared at the card.\n\n\"I nearly canceled again this morning.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"How?\"\n\n\"You checked your phone four times before we left.\"\n\nHe laughed.\n\n\"I guess I'm not subtle.\"\n\nHe leaned against the van.\n\n\"I can show up for Steven, for a friend, for somebody who needs a ride. But when it's something for me, I get scared it'll turn out expensive or complicated or that someone will tell me I've been doing everything wrong.\"\n\n\"Did anyone tell you that today?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Then perhaps the next appointment can be one thing on a calendar, not a verdict on your life.\"\n\nGreg looked at the card again.\n\n\"You're annoyingly sensible.\"\n\n\"I get that a lot.\"\n\nHe held out his hand.\n\n\"Thanks for coming without taking over.\"\n\nI took it.\n\n\"It mattered that you asked.\"\n\nWe drove to the shore and ate sandwiches inside the van while the rain cleared. Greg asked how my next visit to Moonroot was shaping up. I told him the archive had reorganized its public records so nobody needed to invent a reason to ask for a private appointment.\n\n\"Sounds like the kind of place that would love a clipboard.\"\n\n\"You have no idea.\"\n\nHe laughed.\n\nWe'd already talked honestly about my ethical non-monogamy and preference for polyamory. I was never going to be his only source of care, and he didn't have to be mine. Today had been a small example of what we meant: asking, answering, and not turning a favor into an invisible lifetime contract.\n\nHe reached across the console.\n\n\"Can I kiss you?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nThe kiss was sweet and interrupted by the van's windshield wipers coming on unexpectedly. Greg looked at the switch as though it had betrayed him personally.\n\n\"Terrible timing.\"\n\n\"Very you.\"\n\nWe kissed again, a little slower.\n\nAt home he placed the follow-up card on the kitchen board\n\nOn the way home, we stopped at the grocery store for bread.\n\nGreg stood in front of the cereal shelf for a long time.\n\n\"You know what's weird?\" he asked.\n\n\"Many things. Narrow it down.\"\n\n\"I used to think caring for myself was something I had to finish before I deserved to take care of anyone else. Then I'd avoid it because there was always somebody else to help.\"\n\nI picked up the loaf we'd come for.\n\n\"That's a very complicated way to never buy bread.\"\n\nHe laughed.\n\nA woman behind us asked whether the van parked near the side door was ours. A delivery was trying to get through. Greg apologized, moved the van, and returned without offering to unload the truck as penance.\n\n\"Did the world survive?\" I asked.\n\n\"Barely.\"\n\nI handed him the loaf.\n\nWe paid, left the store, and drove home. It was a small day full of ordinary choices: accept a doctor's advice, ask an actual question, move a van without turning it into a performance. None of them made a grand story on their own.\n\nTogether, they felt like the kind of life I wanted to keep making with him., then moved it where he could see it without anyone else reading the details. I put the umbrellas by the door and made tea.\n\nHe sat beside me on the couch, warm and exhausted, and asked whether I would stay that evening.\n\n\"I'd like to.\"\n\nThe rest of the night remained private.\n\nThe next morning, Greg checked the date on the card and set a reminder himself. He didn't make me responsible for remembering.\n\nHe did ask whether I'd join him for breakfast.\n\nI said yes.","word_count":1350},"su-28":{"number":28,"title":"A Song for Nobody Else","content":"The page fell out of Greg's song notebook when I moved it off the porch chair.\n\nI hadn't gone looking. A gust of wind lifted the cover, the paper slipped free, and there was Rose's name in handwriting I knew belonged to a younger Greg.\n\nFor a moment I couldn't move.\n\nIt was a list of unfinished chords and a line about somebody laughing at the sea. Nothing cruel. Nothing secret in any meaningful sense. And still I felt the uncomfortable flash of jealousy that arrived before I could decide what kind of person I wanted to be.\n\nGreg came through the door with two drinks.\n\n\"Hey, you found the notebook.\"\n\nI held up the loose page.\n\n\"It fell out.\"\n\nHe saw the name and stopped.\n\n\"Oh.\"\n\n\"I didn't read the whole thing.\"\n\n\"You're allowed to read it.\"\n\n\"Are you sure?\"\n\nHe set the drinks down.\n\n\"Actually, can I think about that?\"\n\nI nodded and offered the page back. He took it gently, then sat on the step beside the chair.\n\nFor a while we watched the light move over the water.\n\n\"I wrote that after a show,\" he said at last. \"I wasn't very good at finishing songs back then. I thought if I got the ending right, I'd understand what happened.\"\n\n\"You don't owe me an explanation.\"\n\n\"I know. I want to tell you why I kept it.\"\n\nI sat beside him.\n\n\"Okay.\"\n\n\"Because it was part of my life. Because Steven's mom mattered. Because I did some good things and some stupid things, and pretending I don't remember them wouldn't make me a better partner now.\"\n\nI looked down at my hands.\n\n\"That makes sense.\"\n\nHe studied me.\n\n\"Are you upset?\"\n\n\"A little.\"\n\n\"At me?\"\n\n\"Not exactly. I think I'm jealous of the certainty a piece of paper seems to have. It was there before me. It has her name on it. My brain wants to act like there's only room for one person in a notebook.\"\n\nGreg blinked, then laughed softly.\n\n\"Believe me, that notebook has room for way too much.\"\n\nI smiled despite myself.\n\nHe looked out at the sea again.\n\n\"Sometimes I don't know what to do when somebody I love feels like they have to compete with a memory. I don't want to erase Rose. I don't want to hurt you, either.\"\n\n\"I don't want you to erase her.\"\n\n\"Good.\"\n\n\"But I need to know you're not comparing every version of us to a song you never finished.\"\n\nHe considered the question seriously.\n\n\"No,\" he said. \"I'm not. You're not a replacement, Jasper. You make me laugh in different places. You argue with me about the car wash. You tell me when I accidentally volunteer for six jobs. You show up with stories about a square full of lanterns and people who argue over sidewalks.\"\n\n\"Those people do argue.\"\n\n\"I know. I like hearing about them.\"\n\nHe held the page between two fingers.\n\n\"Can I keep this without making it something you have to live up to?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Then I'd like to.\"\n\nI took a breath.\n\nWe had talked about my being ethically non-monogamous and preferring polyamory before we started dating seriously. I'd asked him to accept that the other people in my life would not disappear. It was strange to discover how hard it could still feel when I met the shape of someone who would never be here to explain herself.\n\n\"I guess I have to practice the same generosity,\" I said.\n\n\"You don't have to feel perfect about it.\"\n\n\"Thank goodness.\"\n\nHe smiled and closed the notebook.\n\n\"Want to hear a new piece instead?\"\n\n\"Only if you actually want to play it.\"\n\nHe picked up the guitar.\n\nIt was a small, bright melody that ended too soon. I liked it immediately.\n\n\"Why'd you stop?\"\n\n\"That's all I've got.\"\n\n\"Play it again.\"\n\nHe did.\n\nThe third time, I hummed a low note beneath the ending. He paused.\n\n\"That's not where I thought it was going.\"\n\n\"Good not where or bad not where?\"\n\n\"Interesting.\"\n\nHe made a mark in the margin.\n\nWe were creating something without covering the old page or pretending it meant less than it did. Greg played the melody once more, then put the guitar aside.\n\n\"Should we go for a walk?\" he asked.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nBefore we left, he asked whether I wanted him to put the page away where I wouldn't see it again.\n\n\"No,\" I said. \"I want you to decide where it belongs. I just want to know I can tell you when something catches me off guard.\"\n\nHe looked relieved.\n\n\"That's a deal.\"\n\nHe returned it to the notebook without asking me to watch, then found the small bag of trash we'd forgotten to take out after the last storm.\n\n\"I was going to carry this down to the bins,\" he said. \"Want to come?\"\n\n\"I thought we were going for a romantic walk.\"\n\n\"We can be very romantic while throwing away a torn paper cup.\"\n\nI laughed.\n\nThe path was nearly empty. I carried the lighter bag while Greg carried the one with the broken plastic fragments. At the bins, we sorted them according to the posted rules. He checked the notice twice because he wasn't sure where the foil wrappers went.\n\nIt would have been easier for him to make a joke and leave the bag for someone else to handle. Instead he asked a volunteer and did it properly.\n\nWhen we returned to the boardwalk, Greg brushed sand from his hands.\n\n\"Maybe that's what I want from us,\" he said. \"Not everything neatly resolved. Just being able to ask the weird question without having to run.\"\n\nI reached for his hand.\n\n\"I can try that.\"\n\nHe squeezed once.\n\n\"Me too.\"\n\nWe took the shore path. A small group of children was practicing a beach cleanup by the lower steps, sorting plastic from driftwood under the supervision of a volunteer. Greg stopped to thank them. He didn't make himself the leader; he asked whether they needed another pair of hands. They had enough.\n\n\"Imagine that,\" he said. \"A problem being handled without me.\"\n\n\"A miracle.\"\n\nHe laughed.\n\nAt the far bench, I asked whether I could kiss him. He said yes, and I did, warm and a little awkward when the wind blew his hair across both our faces.\n\nWe walked home holding hands.\n\nThat evening he asked if I wanted to spend the night. I did. We set the notebook safely on its shelf before closing the door.\n\nThe next morning the page was still inside.\n\nBefore breakfast, Steven stopped by to return a borrowed cable.\n\nGreg told him the new melody was coming along. Steven asked if he could listen when it was finished, and Greg said yes.\n\n\"What kind of song?\" Steven asked.\n\n\"Still figuring that out.\"\n\nHe didn't mention Rose's page. The old memory belonged to him, and Steven didn't need to become an audience for my jealousy or for Greg's uncertainty.\n\nAfter Steven left, I asked Greg whether he wanted the new song to have a title.\n\n\"Maybe later. I'm not even sure the bridge is right.\"\n\n\"Then leave it unfinished for now.\"\n\nHe looked at me.\n\n\"You're good at letting things stay complicated.\"\n\n\"I'm practicing.\"\n\nHe laughed and found the notebook where he'd left it.\n\nI took my coffee to the porch. There were places I loved beyond this beach, and people there whose histories I could never entirely know either. I wouldn't ask their pasts to shrink around me just so the present could be easier.\n\nGreg joined me with his guitar. He played the unfinished chord once, then waited for me to hum the note we'd discovered together.\n\nI did.\n\nIt still sounded unusual. We kept it.\n\nGreg showed me where he'd placed it. Not hidden, not displayed as a test. Just part of the book.\n\nI didn't need to be first in every chapter of his life.\n\nI needed the part we were writing together to be real.","word_count":1334},"su-34":{"number":34,"title":"Everyday Magic","content":"The coffee tasted right.\n\nI should have been suspicious.\n\nGreg leaned against the kitchen counter, pretending he hadn't been watching my face as I took the first sip.\n\n\"Well?\"\n\n\"Did you remember how I like it?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Greg.\"\n\n\"Okay, maybe I wrote it down once.\"\n\nI turned toward the fridge. A small square of paper was held up by a magnet shaped like a guitar. Beside the grocery list, in his uneven handwriting, were three words: *Jasper's coffee. Remember.*\n\nI smiled.\n\n\"You're ridiculous.\"\n\n\"Professionally.\"\n\nThe toast was burning.\n\nWe saved half of it.\n\nIt was a weekday morning in Beach City, ordinary enough that the light through the blinds seemed almost too dramatic for the circumstances. A towel hung over the back of a chair. The washing machine made a noise we'd been avoiding for three days. On the table sat a list of things neither of us wanted to do.\n\nGreg stared at the list.\n\n\"Could we just stay here and admire how good we are at making breakfast?\"\n\n\"Only if the laundry does itself.\"\n\nHe sighed.\n\nGreg wrote the tasks on the back of an old set list. Every third item was either underlined twice or surrounded by an enormous question mark.\n\n\"That's not a system,\" I said.\n\n\"That's art.\"\n\n\"Then why does the washing machine have three exclamation points?\"\n\n\"Because it sounds like a drummer trying to escape.\"\n\nI had to admit he was right.\n\nThe calendar on the wall still showed the day we'd gone to Lanternweald together to watch the Starwake lights come on. Greg had drawn a small guitar beside it, then forgotten to erase the reminder when we returned.\n\n\"Do you ever feel like you have to choose one place to call home?\" I asked.\n\nHe looked at the calendar.\n\n\"Sometimes. The van was home for a long time because it could leave whenever I needed it to.\"\n\n\"And now?\"\n\n\"Now I want places where I can stay without deciding I'm stuck. That feels different.\"\n\nI rested my hand on the counter.\n\n\"I want that too. But it's going to take practice when our schedules overlap.\"\n\nHe nodded.\n\n\"Maybe the next thing on the list should be to ask each other before promising the whole day to somebody.\"\n\n\"That's a reasonable thing to write.\"\n\nHe added it in enormous letters.\n\nI looked at him.\n\n\"Not a guitar drawing?\"\n\n\"Don't rush genius.\"\n\nThen he drew a guitar anyway.\n\nWe divided the work without treating it as a grand agreement. He would call the repair shop about the washing machine. I would take the recycling to the collection point and check the schedule for my next Threadway crossing. We would both go grocery shopping because neither trusted the other to remember everything on the list.\n\n\"That last part feels insulting,\" he said.\n\n\"You bought three jars of mustard last week.\"\n\n\"It was on sale.\"\n\n\"We still have two unopened.\"\n\n\"An investment.\"\n\nI laughed.\n\nThe day unraveled by noon.\n\nThe repair shop could not come until Tuesday. The collection point closed early because a delivery had blocked the street. One of the grocery items had been marked incorrectly on the shelf, and the clerk needed to check the price before we paid.\n\nNone of these things was remotely tragic.\n\nGreg behaved as though they were part of an elaborate comedy written specifically to embarrass him.\n\nAt the register, I watched him reach for his wallet to pay for everything. I stopped him.\n\n\"We said we'd split this.\"\n\n\"I know. I just—\"\n\n\"Want to be generous?\"\n\n\"Yeah.\"\n\n\"You can. But asking me whether I'd like you to cover it is different from deciding I can't contribute.\"\n\nHe looked at the groceries.\n\n\"Right. Sorry.\"\n\n\"Can we try again?\"\n\nHe handed me the lighter bag.\n\n\"Would you like to take this one and cover the vegetables?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nThe clerk waited patiently while we sorted it out.\n\nBack home, Greg offered to fix the loose shelf before lunch. I told him we could wait until we had the proper brackets, then put the dry goods in the lower cabinet so the shelf wasn't carrying weight it couldn't hold.\n\nHe looked at the empty space.\n\n\"I was hoping to be the hero of one household repair today.\"\n\n\"You can be the hero of calling the washing-machine technician.\"\n\n\"That's a terrible hero origin.\"\n\nI kissed his cheek.\n\n\"The best ones are.\"\n\nWe ate sandwiches on the porch and watched Steven pass by on his way to meet friends. He stopped long enough to borrow a clean cloth for an art project and returned it before supper.\n\nGreg watched him leave with a soft expression.\n\n\"He's got his own plans.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"I used to get worried when I didn't know every detail.\"\n\n\"Do you still?\"\n\n\"Sometimes. Not as much.\"\n\nI leaned against the railing.\n\n\"That's how I want us to be, too.\"\n\nHe looked at me.\n\n\"How?\"\n\n\"Two people with a life together and other lives of their own. I'm ethically non-monogamous, and I prefer polyamory. You know that. I don't want you guessing that every time I make plans with someone else, I'm taking something from you.\"\n\nHe considered.\n\n\"I don't always like missing you.\"\n\n\"You're allowed to miss me.\"\n\n\"Can I ask for Thursday evening?\"\n\n\"Yes, unless the archive meeting runs long. I'll tell you early if it changes.\"\n\nHe smiled.\n\n\"That's an actual plan.\"\n\n\"Shocking, isn't it?\"\n\nWe finished lunch and walked down to the shore. The sky had turned pale grey. Greg brought the blanket with the old patch. We didn't need it, but he insisted it was part of his beach identity.\n\nAt the water, he asked whether I'd like to dance.\n\n\"There's no music.\"\n\n\"I'm a musician. I can supply it.\"\n\nHe hummed an intentionally terrible tune and held out his hand.\n\nI took it.\n\nWe moved together in the sand until we were laughing too hard to keep the rhythm. He stopped and looked at me, his expression suddenly tender.\n\n\"Can I kiss you?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nThe kiss was warm, unhurried, and made my heart feel much less interested in the list still waiting at home.\n\nLater we returned to the kitchen, wrote down the repair appointment, and ate dinner with the good plates despite having no particular reason to celebrate.\n\nThe washing machine still made its noise.\n\nThe grocery list was already half unfinished again.\n\nBut Greg had remembered the coffee, I'd remembered the vegetables, and neither of us had to pretend ordinary days were beneath the kind of love we wanted.\n\nHe drew a tiny guitar beside Thursday on the calendar. I left it there.\n\nOn Thursday, the laundry technician arrived with a replacement belt and a bill that made Greg whistle.\n\n\"Could I have fixed it myself?\" he asked.\n\n\"Probably not safely with the tools you have,\" the technician replied. \"And the old belt was about to damage the drum.\"\n\nGreg looked at me.\n\n\"Another dramatic rescue prevented by paying someone.\"\n\n\"I know. Terrible for your reputation.\"\n\nHe laughed.\n\nWe kept the repair receipt with the other household records rather than pretending the machine would never fail again. Greg set a reminder to check the filter on the schedule the technician recommended.\n\nThat evening we cooked together. He chopped vegetables while I handled the stove, and we argued over whether an old song counted as dinner music if we both knew it by heart.\n\nHe hummed a few bars of something original instead, deliberately missing a note to make me laugh.\n\nWhen I turned from the counter, he was watching me with a warmth that made the kitchen feel smaller.\n\n\"Would you like to go out?\" he asked.\n\n\"Right now?\"\n\n\"Just a walk. No audience. No heroic repairs.\"\n\nI took his hand.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nWe left the repaired machine humming softly behind us and went to see whether the sea was still there. It was.","word_count":1310},"su-35":{"number":35,"title":"The Note in the Glovebox","content":"The glovebox would not close.\n\nGreg had spent ten minutes trying to make the map fit around an old flashlight, three receipts, a packet of guitar picks, and a folded piece of paper I had never seen before.\n\n\"What's that?\" I asked.\n\nHe froze for half a second.\n\n\"Old stuff.\"\n\n\"Do you want help sorting it?\"\n\n\"Maybe.\"\n\nI didn't reach for the paper.\n\nWe were parked near Beach City, waiting for the rain to ease before driving up to the overlook. The inside of the van smelled of warm vinyl and the faint salt of years spent beside the ocean. Greg opened the glovebox again and lifted out the folded note.\n\n\"I wrote this a long time ago,\" he said.\n\n\"To who?\"\n\n\"Myself, mostly.\"\n\nHe handed it to me.\n\n\"Would you like me to read it?\" I asked.\n\n\"Yeah.\"\n\nThe note was short. It said that if he ever had a home worth coming back to, he should stop pretending he had to leave first. The words were clumsy and heartfelt. It had no signature except his name, written so hard the pen had marked the paper beneath.\n\nI read it twice.\n\n\"When did you write it?\"\n\n\"After one of the times I was pretty sure everything good was about to fall apart.\"\n\nHe looked at the dashboard.\n\n\"I didn't know what to do with it. I stuck it in here and kept finding it every time I tried to clean.\"\n\nI felt a sharp ache in my chest.\n\n\"Why didn't you throw it away?\"\n\n\"Because I kept thinking maybe someday I'd read it and know what it meant.\"\n\nI folded the paper along its old creases.\n\n\"Do you know now?\"\n\n\"Some days.\"\n\nHe looked at me.\n\n\"Other days I still get scared I have to be the funniest guy in the room, the one everybody can count on, or the one nobody asks too much of. Then I see the note and remember I wanted to stop running.\"\n\nI handed it back.\n\n\"I don't want to be the reason you stop traveling.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"I want you to choose when to come back.\"\n\nHe laughed softly.\n\n\"That sounds a lot harder.\"\n\n\"It is.\"\n\nThe rain softened. We carried the old map into a small roadside shelter because the van was too cramped for both of us to spread it across our knees. The paper had marks for places Greg wanted to visit when he was younger, many of them crossed out for reasons he'd forgotten.\n\n\"Did you ever go here?\" I asked, pointing to a town on the coast.\n\n\"No. Ran out of gas money before I got there.\"\n\n\"Do you still want to?\"\n\nHe considered.\n\n\"Maybe. Not because I owe it to that guy who wrote the list. Because it looks nice.\"\n\nI took my own notebook from my bag, the one where I tracked voluntary Threadway visits to Lanternweald and the dates I'd promised people.\n\n\"Want to make a new map?\"\n\n\"With actual plans?\"\n\n\"Some actual plans. Some places we might visit. No promises we can't keep.\"\n\nHe liked that.\n\nGreg tapped the date with his pencil.\n\n\"I told Nola I'd play forty minutes,\" he said. \"Not the whole night. She's got other performers coming.\"\n\n\"Good.\"\n\n\"What if she asks me to stay for cleanup?\"\n\n\"Then you decide whether you're available. Not whether you're a bad person if you leave.\"\n\nHe turned the pencil between his fingers.\n\n\"I used to think leaving was the worst thing I could do to someone. Then I got good at leaving before they asked.\"\n\nI looked toward the van, where the old note had lived for years.\n\n\"Maybe there's a difference between abandoning somebody and finishing what you agreed to do.\"\n\nHe considered that.\n\n\"Forty minutes of music,\" he said. \"And I can still help pack my own gear.\"\n\n\"That's a plan.\"\n\nWe added the return route and the last safe Threadway crossing we knew about. I wrote a question mark beside the time because the Lanternweald tide board could change after heavy rain.\n\nGreg looked at the mark.\n\n\"You're okay with not knowing yet?\"\n\n\"I am if we say that it's uncertain. I don't want to pretend we know something just because the blank part makes us uncomfortable.\"\n\nHe smiled.\n\n\"You'd get along with the people in the archive.\"\n\n\"Some of them are unbearable.\"\n\n\"And you love them anyway.\"\n\nI looked at him.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nHe accepted the answer without needing it softened. Then he drew a star beside the concert and let the rest of the map remain unfinished.\n\nWe marked the next scheduled trip to Starwake Pier, where he had agreed to play a short concert. He wrote a reminder to check whether the stage was available and whether anyone else had already booked it.\n\nThen he pointed to a town on the old map.\n\n\"Could we put that one down as a maybe?\"\n\n\"Absolutely.\"\n\nWe added a little question mark.\n\nHe smiled.\n\n\"I like the maybe.\"\n\n\"Me too.\"\n\nWe sat quietly for a while, watching rain slide down the shelter roof.\n\n\"Can I ask something?\" he said.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Do you ever worry that leaving for another person means I'm going to stop wanting you back?\"\n\nI thought about it.\n\n\"Sometimes I worry you'll interpret my leaving as a judgment about you. But I've told you I'm ethically non-monogamous and prefer polyamory. We both chose this with our eyes open. The answer isn't to stop having other relationships. It's to keep asking what we actually want.\"\n\nHe considered.\n\n\"And what do you want today?\"\n\n\"To finish this map. Then get dinner with you. Then maybe go home and forget the map for a while.\"\n\nHe laughed.\n\n\"Very romantic.\"\n\n\"Terribly.\"\n\nHe moved closer.\n\n\"Can I kiss you?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nWe kissed under the shelter roof while the rain dwindled to a mist. He tasted like coffee, and I felt him smile when I wrapped my arms around him.\n\nWe drove home with the new map folded carefully beside the old note. We didn't cover the past. We made room for it to travel without choosing our destination.\n\nAt home Greg placed the note in his song notebook rather than hiding it in the glovebox again.\n\nBefore he closed the notebook, he showed me the back of the old paper.\n\nThere was a tiny penciled list: a place to sleep, enough money for fuel, one person he could call without rehearsing an apology. He'd crossed out the third line and written it again.\n\n\"I didn't remember that part,\" he said.\n\n\"What do you think it meant?\"\n\n\"That I used to think needing somebody made me a burden.\"\n\nI rested my hand beside his, not on the paper.\n\n\"Do you still think it?\"\n\n\"Some days. Less often when I ask and get a real answer.\"\n\nHe took a breath.\n\n\"Can I ask you for next weekend? Not forever. Just a day.\"\n\n\"Yes. Let's check the calendar before we decide which day.\"\n\nWe did. The Sunday afternoon was free. He wrote it beside the Starwake performance, with enough time between the entries that a delayed crossing wouldn't ruin both plans.\n\nIt wasn't a perfect promise.\n\nIt was one we understood.\n\nGreg closed the notebook and put it on the shelf where he kept the music he was still working on. The old note had stopped being a secret instruction and become part of a story he was willing to read again.\n\nThe map went on the wall.\n\nThe first place marked on it was home.\n\nThe second was a question mark.","word_count":1257}}};
